<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">From deployment to daily work</span><h1>How It Works</h1><p>PocketCat brings tasks, notes, projects and optional Amazon Bedrock assistance into one web workspace. Deployment configuration is handled by the operator; everyday work happens in the user dashboard.</p></section><section class="section"><div class="section-inner">
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">01</span><h2>1. Deploy and configure the workspace</h2><p>The deployment operator provisions a PHP-compatible hosting environment and MySQL database, then completes the initial setup flow to validate runtime requirements, create the application schema and provision the first administrator. Optional service integrations can be configured separately when they are needed.</p><p>This is deployment-time configuration, not a step end users repeat to use PocketCat. After setup, an installation lock prevents the initial configuration flow from being reopened as a public setup page. The exact hosting and operations model depends on the environment running the application.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">02</span><h2>2. Configure services</h2><p>Administrators can configure email and Amazon Bedrock from the control center. Depending on the selected connection, PocketCat uses a Bedrock-compatible API key, AWS Signature Version 4 with IAM credentials, or a separately configured relay.</p><p>Credentials are submitted to authenticated server endpoints, stored through the application's encrypted secret-storage helpers, and used by server-side request code. Saved secret values are not returned to the page as readable credentials. Use least-privilege AWS permissions and select a region and model appropriate to your workload.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">03</span><h2>3. Work in the dashboard</h2><p>Normal users sign in to their own dashboard and can create tasks, notes and projects. The hamburger menu provides the workspace navigation.</p><p>Administrators see the same core workspace tools plus an administration section for managing the installation and users.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">04</span><h2>4. Use Amazon Bedrock when needed</h2><p>AI is optional and is intended to assist with drafting, reviewing and transforming work. An unauthenticated visitor must sign in before using protected AI workflows; the server then checks the account's access and daily allowance.</p><p>The server sends the prompt to the configured Amazon Bedrock endpoint and returns the response to the workspace. Review generated output before relying on it, and do not submit secrets or confidential information unless you have authority to share it with the configured provider.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">05</span><h2>5. Track activity</h2><p>The landing page reads aggregate counters from the database through a lightweight endpoint and refreshes them automatically. Task, note and project creation increments the corresponding activity totals.</p><p>The six-month growth chart uses sample projections, not verified platform activity. The five Live Workspace counters are administrator-configured display values and should not be presented as audited business metrics unless they have been independently validated against production records.</p></article>
</div></section><section class="cta"><span class="eyebrow" style="color:#9db8ff">POCKETCAT</span><h2>Explore the workspace.</h2><p>See the product, create an account or contact the team when you are ready.</p><a class="btn" href="/signup/">Get Started</a></section><?php public_footer(); ?>